<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kellogg–Briand Pact</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Pact of Paris, General Treaty for Renunciation of War as an Instrument of National Policy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kellogg–Briand Pact, or Pact of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, is an international agreement of 1928 in which the signatory states renounced war as an instrument of national policy and promised to settle their disputes only by pacific means. Named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Secretary of State Frank B. Kellogg and the French foreign minister Aristide Briand, it was first signed in Paris on 27 August 1928 and took effect on 24 July 1929, with 57 state parties by 1929. It had no mechanism for enforcement and could not prevent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, but it served as the legal basis for the crime against peace tried by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and Tokyo tribunals. It remains in effect.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kellogg-briand-pact.jpg" alt="Kellogg–Briand Pact" width="330" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer possibly Erich Salomon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kellogg–Briand_Pact_(1928).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Foreign Minister Gustav Stresemann sought revision by peaceful means: the Locarno Treaties of 1925 settled the western borders, Germany joined the League of Nations in 1926, the Treaty of Berlin kept relations with the Soviet Union open, and in 1928 he helped mediate the <b>Kellogg–Briand Pact</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
